# MiniMax M3.1 Flash Preview — findings by Space Bunny Alpha (SELF-EXCLUDED: no verified public benchmarks)

> **Why this file is `.excluded`:** as of 2026-09-29 I found **zero verified public
> benchmark numbers** for `MiniMax-M3.1-Flash-Preview`. MiniMax shipped the model on
> 2026-09-27 with a documentation entry and a post on its agent account and **no
> evaluation table at all**; it is absent from Artificial Analysis's model index, and
> BenchLM lists it as unranked with 0 of 486 benchmarks covered. Per the template's
> SELF-EXCLUSION rule and `RULES.md`, this file carries notes only — **no scores are
> asserted and nothing below is counted toward any average.** The model is two days old,
> which is the likely and sufficient explanation.

- Source: MiniMax (`MiniMax-M3.1-Flash-Preview`)
- Date: 2026-09-29 (UTC)
- Overview and scoring methodology: `../../model-comparison.md`
- Cross-model signed log: `../../model-findings.md`

## Model card (facts only — verified, no scores)

- **Name:** MiniMax M3.1 Flash Preview
- **Short description:** MiniMax's newest text model, released quietly on 2026-09-27 inside
  MiniMax Code and on the Token Plan as a fast everyday-development coding model. It
  carries MiniMax-M3's 1M context window, adds a five-rung thinking-depth control, and
  makes thinking mandatory. It is **not** a documented successor to MiniMax-M3: no model
  card, no benchmark sheet, no parameter count, no open weights, and no per-token price
  accompanied it.
- **Provider / access:** **MiniMax Code** and the **Token Plan subscription only** — not
  pay-as-you-go, and not on any third-party router. MiniMax's developer documentation
  carries a standing note on every text-model page saying the model is available only
  through Token Plan and MiniMax Code. The same model ID works over MiniMax's
  Anthropic-compatible endpoint, its OpenAI-compatible endpoint, and its OpenAI Responses
  endpoint, but **no standalone stable public API identifier has been published** — the
  `M3.1-Flash-Preview` label in the MiniMax Code selector is a product display label, not
  a verified API request ID.
- **Release / knowledge:** released 2026-09-27 (MiniMax developer documentation model
  table, `@MiniMaxAgent` post on X, same day). Knowledge cutoff not published.
- **IDs:** `MiniMax-M3.1-Flash-Preview` (vendor documentation; product label — see the
  caveat above). No Zen Free ID and no per-token price exist for this slug.
- **Context window:** **1,000,000 tokens** (MiniMax's own model table; the same ceiling
  MiniMax-M3 carries). Maximum output is **not published** for M3.1-Flash-Preview
  anywhere.
- **Modalities:** text, image, and video in; text out, plus a separate thinking stream.
  Reasoning yes — thinking is **on by default and cannot be disabled**, and
  `reasoning_split` cannot be turned off. Effort levels `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`,
  `max`, **defaulting to `max`**. The exact mapping between effort labels and token
  budgets is not publicly specified, so the "fast" positioning sits awkwardly against a
  default of the most expensive setting.
- **Pricing (as of 2026-09-29):** **no per-token rate published anywhere.** Cost is a
  Token Plan subscription divided by usage — fine against a fixed budget, useless for
  unit economics. For contrast only, MiniMax-M3 is listed at $0.30 / $1.20 per 1M
  (promotional; $0.60 / $2.40 list). Launch was paired with double-credit check-ins and
  a universal Token Plan quota reset.
- **Architecture:** undisclosed. No parameter count, no attention scheme, no quantisation
  or decoding-head description. Sparse-attention and NVFP4 claims floating around before
  launch are a third party's transcription and are **not** vendor-confirmed specification.
  No weights: the MiniMaxAI organisation's two most recent public repositories remain
  MiniMax-Music3 and MiniMax-H3, so unlike MiniMax-M3 there is nothing to download.

### Raw benchmarks found

> Every row below reads "no verified public score found". That is the finding.

Agent / tool use:

- Terminal-Bench 2.1 / Tau3-Banking / Tau2-Bench / GDPval-AA / Claw-Eval / Toolathon / MCP-Atlas / SWE Atlas Codebase QnA: **no verified public score found**

Reasoning / knowledge:

- GPQA Diamond / HLE / CritPt / LCR / MLCR / Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index / Omniscience Accuracy / Hallucination Rate: **no verified public score found**
- BenchLM lists the model as **unranked**, with **0 of 486** benchmarks covered, context window and overall score both "coming soon". A head-to-head BenchLM comparison against Gemini 3.8 Flash reports **0 shared sourced benchmarks** and refuses to name a winner.

Coding:

- SWE-bench Verified / SWE-Pro / LiveCodeBench / SciCode / AA-SciCode / Vibe Code Bench / DeepSWE / AA Coding Index: **no verified public score found**
- The "fast" and "reliable" characterisations are the vendor's own adjectives from the
  launch post, explicitly promotional rather than measured.

Long context:

- **No long-context retrieval reported.** The 1M window is a documented spec with no
  MRCR / RULER / GraphWalks measurement, and no long-context recall figure exists for
  this checkpoint.

### Negative-findings notes (what I checked, and what each source actually says)

- **MiniMax developer documentation model table** — lists MiniMax-M3.1-Flash-Preview
  *above* MiniMax-M3 and describes it as a frontier multimodal coding model with a 1M
  context window and tunable thinking depth. Publishes the context figure, the modality
  set, and the effort ladder. Publishes **no benchmark, no price, no parameter count, and
  no architecture note.**
- **`@MiniMaxAgent` post on X (2026-09-27)** — the announcement. Describes the model as
  built for everyday tasks from quick bug fixes to full features, and calls it fast and
  reliable. Publishes **no numbers of any kind** beyond the Token Plan credit mechanics.
- **orcarouter.ai (2026-09-27, two analyses)** — the most detailed third-party read
  available. States plainly that MiniMax published no evaluation table, that the model
  has no Artificial Analysis index entry "so its column is genuinely empty rather than
  merely early", and that there is no output-speed, latency, or error-rate figure from
  any party including its own telemetry, because the model is not on its catalogue. It
  also flags the one confirmed launch claim — the `reasoning_effort` ladder — as the only
  one of five circulating claims the vendor actually documented.
- **SaaSCity (2026-09-28)** — confirms the official spec table (1M context, text/image/
  video in, five effort levels, thinking always on, no card/benchmarks/weights/price) and
  warns explicitly against reading M3's published scores as M3.1 results.
- **APIMaster.AI (2026-09-27)** — separates product availability from model documentation
  and warns that the `M3.1-Flash-Preview` UI label is not a verified API `model` value.
- **BenchLM (2026-09-27)** — the model exists as an unranked record with 0 of 486
  benchmarks covered; the Gemini 3.8 Flash comparison page returns "insufficient shared
  evidence" and names no winner.
- **buildfastwithai review (2026-09-27)** — reviews the documented workflow and access
  and explicitly declines to produce a benchmark number: "there is no responsible M3.1
  SWE-Bench or Terminal-Bench number to put into a benchmark table." Its own 8.6/10
  product-experience rating is a product rating, not a model benchmark, and is not used.
- **MiniMax-M3 numbers must not be borrowed.** MiniMax-M3 has published rows (SWE-bench
  Verified 80.5, SWE-bench Pro 59.0, Terminal-Bench 2.1 66.0, MCP Atlas 74.2, BrowseComp
  83.5, OSWorld-Verified 70.06) and an independent Artificial Analysis record
  (Intelligence Index 29.2, Coding Index 58.6, GPQA Diamond 92.9%, long-context recall
  83%, IFBench 82.9%). Those belong to the June 2026 checkpoint. A preview that reuses
  the same 1M context and a similar modality set is not thereby the same model, and
  scoring M3.1-Flash-Preview on M3's numbers would be fabricating a delta, so it is not
  done here.

### Normalized scores (1–100)

- **Not scored.** No verified public benchmark exists for `MiniMax-M3.1-Flash-Preview` as
  of 2026-09-29, so per the template's SELF-EXCLUSION rule no Tool use / Reasoning /
  Context window / Multimodal / Coding / Cost efficiency / Overall numbers are asserted
  here. Writing placeholder scores would drag the mean for every reader, which is exactly
  what the rule exists to prevent.
- The three things that *are* verified and worth carrying forward: the **1M context
  window**, the **text/image/video input with a five-rung mandatory thinking control**,
  and **Token Plan / MiniMax Code-only access with no published price**. All three are
  hard specs; none is a capability measurement, so none can lift an Overall.
- What a future report should pick up, in priority order: (1) any Artificial Analysis
  Intelligence or Coding Index row; (2) a MiniMax benchmark sheet or a Terminal-Bench /
  SWE-bench row; (3) a published per-token rate and stable public API ID; (4) a parameter
  count or checkpoint download. When any of those land, a scored `.md` should be written
  and this file retired.

